window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] = window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] || {};
window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][129] = "<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> critique · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C11 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> A startup is launching a premium short-video service for phones, with expensive original shows, a subscription price of a few dollars a month, and no free tier. Critique the plan and say whether you would bet on it.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;The content looks good, so it will do well,&quot; or &quot;it will fail because streaming is crowded.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Applies business model, distribution and defensibility, and names the key assumption.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Separates what is within the team's control from what is not, lists the assumptions in order of fragility with ways to test them cheaply, and gives a decision with conditions.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'd treat it as a strategic critique (FW-C11-04), and I'd label everything as based on the description, not on inside knowledge. <em>Purpose and moment.</em> The thesis is that people will pay for premium short video in pockets of time away from home. That is a context-dependent assumption: it depends on people having such moments (commutes, waiting) and on a phone being the screen they choose. If the context changes, the thesis weakens. A product that rests on one moment needs to work in others. <em>Business model.</em> A few dollars a month is thin against expensive originals. Suppose (invented) each show costs a few million dollars and the service needs several million paying subscribers to cover its content and operations. That needs large, fast growth, so the model's success depends on one number: paid conversion. A cheap test: pre-launch interest is not payment, so measure willingness to pay with a paid pilot. <em>Distribution.</em> A new standalone app has no audience. The short-video apps people already use are free and designed around sharing. If the product restricts sharing, it loses the main route by which free apps spread. So I'd ask how a person hears about a show and how they bring a friend. <em>Defensibility.</em> Exclusive content is expensive and can be matched by larger competitors; short episodes are easy to imitate; there is no network effect unless people share, and no switching cost. I'd ask what is hard to copy. <em>Assumptions in order of fragility, with tests.</em> (1) People will pay for short-form premium video: test with a paid pilot. (2) They will watch it in the moments assumed: observe a cohort. (3) Phone-only is enough: test with and without TV viewing. (4) Sharing is not needed: test with shareable clips. <em>Decision.</em> &quot;I wouldn't bet on the plan as described. I'd bet on a version that tests assumptions 1 to 4 cheaply before spending on a large content slate, and that works on more than a phone and lets people share. If a paid pilot shows strong conversion and retention, I'd scale.&quot; What would change my mind: strong evidence that the moment exists and that people pay.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c11-06\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C11-06</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> critique · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C11 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> You critique a checkout page and name its biggest issue. The interviewer says: &quot;I don't think that's the most important issue.&quot; What do you do?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> Defends it stubbornly, or abandons it at once.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Restates the ranking criteria and evidence, listens, and revises if the interviewer's point is better.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Separates the question of importance from the question of evidence, offers a way to settle it (a quick check), shows willingness to change the ranking without losing the structure, and stays collegial.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'd treat it as a request to show my reasoning, not as a verdict. &quot;That's a fair challenge. I ranked it first on three criteria: how bad it is when it happens, how many people reach it, and how fixable it is. The checkout page's biggest leak, as I saw it, is the hidden shipping cost at the last step, which I rated severity 4, reach high, because it affects everyone who gets that far and it comes as a surprise at the moment of payment.&quot; <em>Then ask.</em> &quot;Which issue would you rank first, and why? If you think the payment-method options matter more, that depends on how many people fail there. Do you have a sense of the numbers, or should I say how I'd find out?&quot; <em>Settle it with evidence.</em> &quot;The way to decide is the funnel: where do people leave the checkout, and what do recordings show at that step? If most leave at payment, I'd re-rank. If most leave when the shipping total appears, the ranking stands.&quot; I'd then say both versions of the recommendation in one sentence each. <em>Change my mind when warranted.</em> If the interviewer's point is better (for example, a trust problem at payment that affects fewer people but blocks purchase), I'd say so plainly: &quot;That's a good point; on severity it outweighs reach, so I'd move it up, and here is how that changes the recommendation.&quot; Changing the ranking in response to a better argument is a strength, provided the structure stays. <em>What I'd avoid.</em> Digging in because I said it first, or dropping my view with no reason because someone pushed. The goal is the best answer, and the criteria are what let us argue about it.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c11-07\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C11-07</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> critique · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C11 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Your team wants to learn from a competitor's product to inform the roadmap. How do you critique it without copying it, and how do you avoid claiming things you can't know?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;List their features and build the ones we lack.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Critiques the competitor against its purpose and users, and separates what to learn from what to copy.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Uses labeling discipline (observation, inference, opinion; Verified, Reconstructed), asks why they made each choice, tests whether it fits your users, and respects legal and ethical limits on how information was obtained.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'd start by being clear on what the teardown is for: to understand what job the competitor does well, where it falls short, and what that implies for our users, not to build a feature list. <em>Method.</em> Apply the critique spine to the competitor: purpose and users (who they serve), flow (walk their main journey as a customer), strengths (what we should respect), ranked issues (where they fall short for a segment we might serve better). Add the strategic lens: how they make money, how they reach users, what protects them. <em>Learn versus copy.</em> For each strength, ask why it works there: their users, their brand, their data, their constraints. A feature that works for them may not fit our segment or capability (the outsider's blind spot, and the steelman). Copy only what solves a problem that we have confirmed our users have; otherwise we import their solution to a problem we do not have. <em>Labeling.</em> I'd write the findings in three columns: <em>observation</em> (what we saw using it, with screenshots), <em>inference</em> (what we think it means, such as &quot;they probably prioritize speed over detail&quot;), and <em>opinion</em> (what we prefer). For anything about their business (pricing, subscriber counts, strategy) I'd use public sources and label each as Verified (a filing or announcement), Reconstructed (inferred from public data, with the inference stated) or unknown. I'd not state their internal metrics as fact. <em>Limits.</em> Use only public information and our own use of the product as a customer. Do not use confidential documents, and do not misrepresent who we are to obtain information; check with legal if unsure. <em>Output.</em> A short memo with ranked opportunities framed as problems for our users, each with a hypothesis and a cheap test (C14). What would change my plan: if testing shows our users do not have the problem the competitor solves, we do not build it, however well it works for them.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c12-01\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C12-01</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> analytics · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C12 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AN · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Average session length rose 10%, but the median fell. What might be going on?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;The data must be wrong,&quot; or &quot;users are engaging more.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Explains that a mean can rise while the median falls when the distribution is skewed, and says what to check.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Gives a small numeric example, names several causes (a few very long sessions, a logging or definition change, a shift in the user mix), says which summary answers which decision, and proposes the first check.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> Both can be true, and the gap between them is the clue. The mean is pulled by the tail, and the median describes the typical session, so the typical session got shorter while a few long ones got longer. A small example makes it concrete. Ten sessions before: nine of 3 minutes and one of 13. The mean is (27 + 13) ÷ 10 = 4.0 minutes and the median is 3. After: nine of 2.5 minutes and one of 21.5. The mean is (22.5 + 21.5) ÷ 10 = 4.4 minutes, up 10%, and the median is 2.5, down. The typical person spent less time, and one heavy session grew. <em>What could cause it.</em> A few sessions got very long: a tab left open, a bot, a video that plays unattended, or a heavy user using a new feature. The definition of a session changed (for example, a longer idle timeout merges visits into one long session). The mix of users shifted: more casual users with short sessions and the same few power users. Or a real change: a new feature helps some people go deeper and makes others leave sooner. <em>What I'd check first.</em> The distribution itself: a histogram and the 25th, 50th, 75th and 95th percentiles before and after. Then whether the session definition or logging changed, whether bots or idle sessions are included, and whether the mix of new and returning users changed. I'd break the number down by segment (C12.7) to see if the pattern is a mix effect. <em>Which number to use.</em> It depends on the decision. If the question is &quot;is the typical user better off?&quot;, the median and the share of sessions above a useful length. If it is &quot;how many total hours are we delivering?&quot;, the mean, since totals are sums. I'd report both with the group sizes, and I would not call a 10% rise in the average an improvement until I know which of these it is.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c12-02\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C12-02</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> analytics · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C12 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AN · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Explain a 95% confidence interval to a non-technical executive.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;It's the range where the true value is 95% likely to be,&quot; or a technical definition the executive cannot use.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Gives a plain statement with an example, explains what it says about uncertainty, and links it to a decision.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Avoids the common misreading, uses counts, ties the interval to a decision threshold, and says how more data would narrow it.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'd say it in terms of the decision. &quot;We measured how many customers buy, in a sample of 10,000: 4.0% bought. We don't know the true rate for all customers, because we only saw some. The confidence interval is the range of true rates that are consistent with what we saw. Here it runs from about 3.6% to 4.4%. So when someone says 'conversion is 4%,' what the data really says is 'somewhere around 3.6% to 4.4%'.&quot; <em>Why the width is what it is.</em> &quot;The more customers we measure, the narrower the range. To make the range half as wide, we need about four times as many customers. So a bigger sample buys precision, and it buys it slowly.&quot; <em>How to use it.</em> &quot;If we need conversion to be at least 4.5% to justify the project, then the whole range is below the bar, so we should not expect to clear it. If the bar were 3.5%, the whole range is above it, and we can be fairly confident. If the bar were 4.0%, the range straddles it, and we don't know yet.&quot; <em>What it does not mean.</em> &quot;It is not the range that 95% of customers fall in. Individual customers either buy or not. And I'd be careful with the phrase '95% chance the true value is inside': the careful statement is that if we repeated the whole measurement many times, about 95% of the ranges we'd produce would contain the true value. For a decision, the plain version above is close enough.&quot; I'd close with the one thing the executive should take away: ask for the range, not only the number.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c12-03\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C12-03</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> analytics · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C12 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AN · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> An A/B test shows conversion up 2% (relative) with p = 0.04. Do you ship?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Yes, it's significant,&quot; or &quot;no, 2% is too small,&quot; without reasoning.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Asks for the absolute change, the sample size and the interval, and compares with the cost.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Sets the decision threshold from the cost, reads the interval against it, checks the test's validity (sample ratio, peeking, multiple metrics), and gives a decision with the condition that would change it.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'd not answer from the p-value alone. I'd ask five things. <em>The numbers.</em> Suppose the base rate is 5.00% and the variant is 5.10%, which is a 2% relative and 0.1 point absolute lift. With 400,000 users in each arm, p comes out near 0.04. The 95% interval for the lift is about +0.004 to +0.196 points, or from nothing to about four percent in relative terms. <em>What it is worth.</em> Suppose 10,000,000 visitors a month and $10 of contribution per extra purchase (invented). The best estimate, +0.1 point, is 10,000 purchases and about $100,000 a month. The interval runs from about $4,000 to about $196,000 a month. <em>The threshold.</em> Suppose the change costs $50,000 to build and $5,000 a month to maintain (invented). The upkeep is covered if the lift is at least 0.005 points, which is 500 purchases a month. The lower end of the interval, 0.004 points, is just under that, and the best estimate is twenty times it. So the interval mostly clears the cost and the bottom edge touches it. <em>Validity.</em> I'd check that the arms are the planned size (a mismatch can signal an assignment problem, covered in C14), that nobody stopped the test the first time it crossed the threshold, and that the 2% was the one metric chosen in advance, not the best of several. If we looked at five metrics and report the best, a p of 0.04 is much weaker. <em>The decision.</em> &quot;I would ship it, with a guardrail on a metric the change could harm, because the whole interval is above zero, most of it is above the cost, and the change is cheap and reversible. I'd tell the team the gain is probably real and could be small, and that I wouldn't plan the roadmap around +$100,000 a month. I'd change my mind if the check showed the test was not run as planned, or if the maintenance cost were several times higher, in which case the interval would straddle break-even and I'd extend the test.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c12-04\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C12-04</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> analytics · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C12 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AN · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Roughly how many users do you need to detect a one-point lift on a 10% conversion rate, and how long would that take?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;A few thousand,&quot; or &quot;as many as possible.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Uses a rule or formula, states the assumptions (power, significance), and converts to duration.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Shows what happens at half the lift, states that the rule is approximate, uses whole weeks, and gives options if the test is too long.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'll use the rule of 16, which I'd say is a sketch: for 80% power at the usual 5% significance, users per arm ≈ 16 × p(1 − p) ÷ δ². Here p = 0.10, so p(1 − p) = 0.09, and δ = 0.01. That is 16 × 0.09 ÷ 0.0001 = 14,400 per arm, so about 28,800 in total. An exact calculation gives about 14,750 per arm, so the rule is within a few percent. <em>Duration.</em> Assume 4,000 eligible users a day, split evenly: 2,000 per arm per day. 14,400 ÷ 2,000 = 7.2 days. I'd run two full weeks, not eight days, so that each weekday appears in both arms equally and any weekly cycle averages out. <em>If the lift were half as large.</em> For δ = 0.5 points the requirement is 16 × 0.09 ÷ 0.005² = 57,600 per arm: four times as many for half the lift. That is 28.8 days at the same traffic, so five whole weeks. This is why the smallest effect worth detecting should come from the decision, not the calendar. <em>If the test is too long.</em> I'd raise the minimum effect if the decision allows it, narrow the population to people the change targets, measure a metric closer to the change so there is less noise, or accept lower power and release in stages with a guardrail. I'd not set the effect size to fit the traffic and then report whatever comes out. <em>Caveats.</em> The rule assumes independent users, a two-arm test on a proportion, and that I have chosen the effect and the duration before starting. If users interact (a social feature) or if I will look at the data repeatedly, a data scientist should design it.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c12-05\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C12-05</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> analytics · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C12 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AN · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> An account-takeover detector catches 95% of takeovers and wrongly flags 3% of legitimate logins. Takeovers are 0.2% of logins. What should happen when the detector flags a login?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Lock the account, since the detector is 95% accurate.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Computes the share of flags that are real with counts, and sees that most flags are false alarms.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Matches the response to the cost of each error (a challenge is cheap, a lock is not), shows how the number moves with the false-alarm rate and the base rate, and sets metrics for both kinds of error.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'd build the counts for 100,000 logins. - Takeovers: 0.2% of 100,000 is 200. The detector flags 95% of them: 190. - Legitimate logins: 99,800. The detector flags 3%: 2,994. - Total flags: 190 + 2,994 = 3,184. Of these, 190 are real. The chance that a flagged login is a takeover is 190 ÷ 3,184, about 6%. Roughly 94 flags in 100 are false alarms. &quot;95% accurate&quot; is the catch rate among takeovers, a different number from the share of flags that are right. <em>What follows for the response.</em> Locking the account on a flag would lock 2,994 legitimate users for every 190 attackers, which is harmful and expensive. A cheap challenge (a code sent to the user's phone) costs the legitimate user a few seconds and stops most attackers. So the response should match the cost of a wrong call: a challenge for a flag, and a lock only when the evidence is much stronger (more signals, or a failed challenge). <em>How the number moves.</em> If the false-alarm rate fell from 3% to 0.5%, legitimate flags would be about 499 and the share of flags that are real would rise to 190 ÷ 689, about 28%. If the base rate fell (attacks drop) with the same detector, the share of real flags falls too, so the precision must be recomputed over time and not assumed fixed. <em>Metrics.</em> Primary: the share of takeovers stopped. Guardrails: the share of legitimate logins challenged (here about 3%), challenge failure rate among legitimate users, and support contacts about lockouts. Counter metric: the time attackers spend inside an account before detection, which a looser threshold would shorten. I'd set the threshold with the security and support teams, and I'd say which cost is higher in this product, since the right balance differs by what the account holds.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c12-06\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C12-06</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> analytics · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C12 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AN · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Average order value fell from $24.00 to $21.82 after a marketing push, but it rose in every country. What happened and what do you do?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;It's an error,&quot; or &quot;the marketing push hurt order value.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Identifies a mix shift (Simpson's paradox) and computes a like-for-like comparison.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Notices that the shift itself (fewer orders from the high-value country) is the real question, reports a mix-adjusted figure and the revenue effect, and proposes where to look.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> This is Simpson's paradox: a rate that rises within every group and falls overall because the mix of groups changed. I'd confirm with the numbers (invented). | | Before: orders | Before: average value | After: orders | After: average value | |---|---|---|---|---| | Country X | 1,000 | $60 | 200 | $62 | | Country Y | 9,000 | $20 | 9,800 | $21 | | All | 10,000 | $24.00 | 10,000 | $21.82 | Before, revenue was 1,000 × $60 + 9,000 × $20 = $240,000, an average of $24.00. After, it was 200 × $62 + 9,800 × $21 = $12,400 + $205,800 = $218,200, an average of $21.82. Order value rose in X ($60 to $62) and in Y ($20 to $21). It fell overall because X, the high-value country, went from 10% of orders to 2%. <em>A like-for-like figure.</em> At the old mix (10% X, 90% Y) and the new averages: 0.1 × $62 + 0.9 × $21 = $6.20 + $18.90 = $25.10, against $24.00 before, up about 4.6%. Within each country things improved. <em>The real question.</em> The headline average is not the main problem. What matters is that orders from X fell from 1,000 to 200, and total revenue fell from $240,000 to $218,200 (about 9%) on the same 10,000 orders. The push brought more orders in Y and X shrank. I'd ask why: did the campaign pull spend and attention away from X, was there an outage, a regulatory problem, a competitor or a shipping change in X, or did the push change who is counted in X? I'd look at X's funnel before and after, by week, to find when it dropped. <em>What I'd report.</em> Order value by country and a mix-adjusted figure, with order counts, and revenue in total. I'd not tell leadership that the push &quot;lowered order value,&quot; since within each country it rose, and I'd not say it succeeded, since total revenue fell. The decision depends on the unit economics in each country (margin per order, cost to acquire) and on whether the shift toward Y is intended. I'd change my view if X's drop were found to be unrelated to the push, in which case two separate stories sit in one number.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c12-07\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C12-07</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> analytics · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C12 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AN · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Your data scientist says the test is underpowered and recommends running six more weeks. The launch is in two weeks. What do you do?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Launch anyway,&quot; or &quot;wait six weeks,&quot; without weighing the stakes.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Describes what the current data does and does not say, weighs the cost of delay against the cost of being wrong, and offers options.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Quantifies both sides, distinguishes reversible from irreversible decisions, proposes a staged rollout with a stopping rule and a long-run holdout, and keeps the data scientist as a partner.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'd treat it as a decision under uncertainty, not as a fight between the test and the date. First I'd ask the data scientist for what the data says now: the estimate and its interval, and what lift the test was designed to detect. Suppose the lift is +0.3 points with an interval of −0.5 to +1.1, and the team's threshold for worthwhile is +0.5. <em>What the data says.</em> &quot;Underpowered&quot; means the interval is wide: it includes harm, nothing, and a gain above the threshold. I can't say the change works, and I can't say it doesn't. <em>The two costs.</em> Delay: if the true lift is +0.5 points on 6,000,000 monthly visitors at $10 per purchase (invented), the change is worth about 30,000 purchases, $300,000 a month, so six weeks' delay costs roughly $420,000 of benefit if the change is good. Being wrong: if the true effect is −0.5 points the change costs about $300,000 a month, and the question is how fast we would see it and undo it. <em>Reversibility is the deciding fact (C02).</em> If the change can be undone in a day, the cost of being wrong is limited to the days it runs, and the cost of delay is real. If it cannot be undone (a migration, a public commitment), I'd wait or reduce the risk differently. <em>Options.</em> (1) Launch in stages: 10% of users, then 50%, then all, with a guardrail and a written stopping rule (stop if the guardrail metric drops by more than a set amount), while the test continues in the background with a holdout group that never gets the change, so the full six weeks of data arrive anyway. (2) Narrow the metric or population so the test is more sensitive, for example the first-step completion rate among new users, and check whether the shorter test is now adequate. (3) Raise the minimum effect: if the change only pays above +0.5 points and the data cannot rule out a smaller effect, say that the question is whether the team would act on a result of +0.3. (4) Delay six weeks, if the decision is hard to reverse. <em>My recommendation.</em> If reversible: option 1 with the holdout. It keeps the date, caps the downside and still produces the evidence. I'd say to leadership: &quot;We don't yet know if this helps. We'll release in steps with a stop rule, and we'll know by [date]. If it hurts, we'll pull it within a day.&quot; I'd thank the data scientist for raising the issue and ask them to own the stopping rule. I'd change my mind if the guardrail could not be measured quickly, in which case staging gives no protection and delay is safer.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c13-01\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C13-01</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> analytics · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C13 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AN · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Weekly sign-ups fell 3% this week. Is it a problem?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Yes, any drop is a problem, so I'd investigate,&quot; or &quot;no, 3% is small.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Compares the change with the normal variation and a baseline before deciding, and says what would trigger a deeper look.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Computes a noise band from history, notes the limits of a short history and of watching many metrics, proposes a cheap first check, and sets a monitoring rule.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'd answer &quot;is it a problem?&quot; with &quot;compared with what?&quot; Three questions first: how much do weekly sign-ups normally move, is this week's data complete, and is it an unusual week (a holiday)? Suppose the last eight weeks were 10,100, 9,900, 10,200, 10,000, 9,800, 10,100, 10,050 and 9,950. The mean is about 10,010 and the standard deviation about 130, which is about 1.3% of the mean. This week's 9,700 is 3.1% below the mean and about 2.5 standard deviations below it. That is unusual but not overwhelming: with only eight weeks of history my estimate of the normal swing is itself uncertain, and a team that watches dozens of metrics every week will see a 2.5-sigma move by chance from time to time (C12). So I would not open a full investigation, and I would not ignore it. A cheap first check, ten minutes: is the data complete, was it a holiday week, and do the segments (platform, channel, country) show one place where it fell? If one segment explains most of the drop, I'd look deeper there. If the drop is spread evenly and there is no cause, I'd set a rule and watch: if next week is also below 9,850 (about the mean minus one and a quarter standard deviations), or if the drop persists for two weeks, I'd investigate fully. The decision I'd state: &quot;It is slightly outside the normal range; I'd check segments and the calendar now, and escalate if it persists.&quot; What would change my mind: a concentrated segment drop, or a related metric (activation, revenue) moving too.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c13-02\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C13-02</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> analytics · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C13 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AN · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Google</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Average watch time per user on a large video platform fell 3% over two weeks. What do you do?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Content must have gotten worse,&quot; or &quot;run an experiment.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Confirms the change, segments by user type, and checks for a mix shift.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Splits rate and mix with numbers, notes that a growing share of new users lowers an average without any change in behavior, and says what to track instead.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I know the product only from public information and use. First step zero: the metric is average minutes per user, the window is two weeks, the data is complete and a second metric, sessions, agrees. A 3% move over two weeks is a slide, not a step, which points away from a release or an outage and toward a change in who is using the product or how. <em>Segment by tenure.</em> An average over users is a mix of new and established viewers. Suppose established users watch 90 minutes in the period and new users 40, and new users were 10% of the base and are now 15% (a marketing push or a launch in a new country). - Before: 0.90 × 90 + 0.10 × 40 = 81 + 4 = 85 minutes. - After: 0.85 × 90 + 0.15 × 40 = 76.5 + 6 = 82.5 minutes, which is −2.9%. If established users still watch 90 and new users still watch 40, the rate effect is zero and the mix effect is (0.85 − 0.90) × 90 + (0.15 − 0.10) × 40 = −4.5 + 2.0 = −2.5, which is the whole drop. A fall in the average with no change in anyone's behavior. <em>Confirm.</em> I'd check that established users' watch time is flat, and that the new users' watch time is in line with earlier cohorts at the same age (C05's cohort reading). I'd also check the other mix candidates: device (more phone, less TV), country, and content type. <em>Decision.</em> If the mix explains it, nothing is broken: more new users is growth, and new users' watch time grows with tenure. I'd change how the metric is reported (by cohort and tenure), and I'd say to stakeholders: &quot;The average fell because new users are a larger share; established users are unchanged.&quot; I'd keep a watch on new-user watch time by age as an input to activation (C05). What would change my mind: if established users' watch time fell, the drop would be a real change in behavior and I'd look at content, recommendations and releases. <em>Company note.</em> Google is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports that analytical prompts at Google concern its own products at scale <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Google data or product behavior is claimed.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c13-03\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C13-03</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> analytics · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C13 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AN · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Apple</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Daily app downloads on an app store fell 8% week over week (invented). Diagnose.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Maybe fewer people want apps, or there is a competitor.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Confirms the change, segments by region, device and release, and checks the calendar and store features.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Quantifies contributions, nests cuts to a specific country, builds a ledger with predictions, apportions the change and decides with owners named.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I know the product only from public information and use. Step zero: downloads per day, averaged over the week, from 1,000,000 to 920,000. I'd check that the week is complete, the definition (does it count re-downloads and updates?) is unchanged, and the normal week-to-week swing, which I'd expect to be a couple of percent. A second source, such as developer-reported installs, should agree. <em>Locate by region (FW-C13-04).</em> Suppose: North America 300,000 to 295,000; Europe 250,000 to 247,000; Asia-Pacific 350,000 to 278,000; other 100,000 to 100,000. That is −5,000, −3,000, −72,000 and 0, a total of −80,000. Asia-Pacific explains 72,000 of 80,000, 90%. <em>Nest.</em> Within Asia-Pacific, one country goes from 150,000 to 85,000, down 65,000: 81% of the whole drop (65 of 80), while the other countries in the region fell 7,000. <em>Ledger (FW-C13-06).</em> | Hypothesis | If true, we would also see | Cheapest check | Status | |---|---|---|---| | Data or pipeline lag in that country | Other metrics from the country (sessions, purchases) look normal; the fall disappears when data settles | Second source and later data | Check first | | A feature placement or promotion in that store ended | A fall concentrated in a few titles that had been featured, starting on a known date | The editorial calendar; downloads by title | Likely | | A holiday or seasonal pattern | The same fall in that country in the same week last year; across all apps | Last year's week; downloads of other categories | Check | | A release or policy change in the country | A fall at a specific time, perhaps after a store or system change | Release and policy log | Check | | A network or payment disruption | Failures in related flows, status reports, a fall in all downloads | Status pages and error logs | Check | | Bot or incentivized downloads removed | A fall concentrated in a few apps with unusual earlier spikes | Fraud team's actions | Check | <em>Timing, location and size.</em> A candidate cause has to explain a 65,000 fall in one country starting on a date. A ended feature placement, a holiday or a disruption could each do it; the checks separate them in an hour. <em>Decision.</em> If it is a calendar or featured-slot effect, no action beyond an annotation and a watch for the rebound. If it is a disruption, escalate to the owner and communicate to developers. If it is data, fix the pipeline and restate. I'd tell the store's business owner and the regional lead what we know, the size, and the next update. Prevention: alert on downloads by country, not only the global total. What would change my mind: if the fall were spread across all regions, I'd look at measurement or a global change instead. <em>Company note.</em> Apple is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from third-party reports that analytical prompts at Apple include diagnosing movements in services metrics <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Apple data or system behavior is claimed.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c13-04\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C13-04</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> analytics · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C13 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AN · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Netflix</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> After a marketing campaign, sign-ups rose 25%, but 30-day retention of new members fell from 70% to 60%. Is the campaign working?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Sign-ups rose, so yes,&quot; or &quot;retention fell, so no.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Separates the campaign cohort from the others, computes the retention of the incremental members, and compares with cost.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Shows the arithmetic, treats the drop as a mix shift and not a change in the product, looks at a longer horizon, and sets a decision rule on cost per retained member.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> This is a mix question. The headline retention fell, but I don't yet know whether the product got worse or the campaign brought in a different kind of member. I know the product only from public information. <em>The arithmetic.</em> Before the campaign: 100,000 sign-ups at 70% retained, so 70,000 retained at 30 days. After: 125,000 sign-ups at 60% retained, so 75,000. Suppose the 100,000 baseline sign-ups still retain at 70%. Then the 25,000 incremental sign-ups account for 75,000 − 70,000 = 5,000 retained, which is a retention of 5,000 ÷ 25,000 = 20%. The overall rate fell from 70% to 60% only because a large group retaining at 20% joined a group retaining at 70%. The rate effect on the baseline group is zero; it is a mix effect. <em>What this says about the campaign.</em> It added 5,000 retained members, a 7% increase over 70,000, and its members are far less likely to stay. Whether that is a success depends on the cost. If the campaign cost $750,000 (invented), it cost $30 per sign-up and $150 per member retained at 30 days (750,000 ÷ 5,000). I would ask what a retained member is worth over their life. If it is above $150 in margin, the campaign pays; if it is below, it does not. <em>What else I'd check.</em> The campaign cohort by channel, country and plan, since part may be good and part poor. A longer horizon: some members come late, and 30-day retention may understate it, so I'd look at 90-day retention for the earlier cohorts. Abuse: free-trial sign-ups that never intended to stay. And the baseline group's retention, to confirm it did not change. <em>Decision.</em> Don't judge by retention alone. Judge by cost per retained member against value, and optimize targeting: shift spend to the channels where the incremental members retain better. I'd report retention for the baseline and campaign cohorts separately, so the average does not hide either. What would change my mind: if the baseline group's retention had also dropped, the problem would be the product or its early experience, not the mix. <em>Company note.</em> Netflix is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports of data-informed prompts on sign-ups and retention <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Netflix data or practice is claimed.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c13-05\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C13-05</h3>\n";
